K.st('ch01-st1', 'map skills such as grid references, scale and direction',
     terms=[('latitude', 'the angular distance of a place north or south of the equator, measured in degrees from 0° to 90°'),
            ('longitude', 'the angular distance of a place east or west of the prime meridian at Greenwich, measured from 0° to 180°'),
            ('scale', 'the ratio between a distance on a map and the same distance on the ground'),
            ('grid reference', 'a set of numbers, eastings first and then northings, that locates a square or point on a map'),
            ('compass bearing', 'a direction measured in degrees clockwise from north, from 000° to 360°'),
            ('contour line', 'a line on a map joining places of equal height above sea level')],
     facts=[('Which line of latitude is at 0°?', 'The equator', ['The prime meridian', 'The Tropic of Cancer', 'The Arctic Circle'], 'Latitude is measured north and south from the equator (0°).'),
            ('Through which city does the prime meridian (0° longitude) pass?', 'London (Greenwich)', ['Paris', 'New York', 'Cairo'], 'The prime meridian was fixed at Greenwich by the International Meridian Conference of 1884.'),
            ('When giving a grid reference, which number is written first?', 'The easting', ['The northing', 'The height', 'The bearing'], 'Remember "along the corridor and up the stairs": eastings first.'),
            ('What compass bearing is due south-west?', '225°', ['135°', '315°', '45°'], 'Bearings run clockwise from north: S = 180°, W = 270°, SW = 225°.'),
            ('On a map, contour lines that are very close together show what?', 'A steep slope', ['Flat land', 'A river', 'A gentle slope'], 'Height changes quickly over a short distance.')],
     why=[('Explain why eastings are always given before northings in a grid reference.', 'It is an agreed convention: if everyone reads along (east) first and then up (north), each grid reference identifies only one square, so map users cannot confuse two different locations.'),
          ('Explain why a six-figure grid reference is more useful than a four-figure grid reference for locating a building.', 'A four-figure reference only identifies a 1 km square; a six-figure reference divides the square into tenths in each direction and locates a point to within about 100 m.'),
          ('Explain why a 1:25 000 map shows more detail than a 1:250 000 map.', 'It is a larger scale: 1 cm represents only 250 m rather than 2.5 km, so each area is drawn bigger and small features such as footpaths and buildings can be shown.'),
          ('Explain why lines of longitude are used to work out time differences.', 'The Earth rotates 360° in 24 hours, so the Sun appears to move 15° of longitude each hour; places further east see sunrise and noon earlier.')],
     contrast=[('latitude', 'longitude', 'Latitude measures distance north or south of the equator (lines run east–west); longitude measures distance east or west of the prime meridian (lines run north–south and meet at the poles).'),
               ('a large-scale map', 'a small-scale map', 'A large-scale map (e.g. 1:10 000) shows a small area in great detail; a small-scale map (e.g. 1:1 000 000) shows a large area with little detail.')],
     misc=[('A 1:50 000 map is a larger scale than a 1:25 000 map because 50 000 is a bigger number.', '1:25 000 is the larger scale: the representative fraction 1/25 000 is bigger, so features are drawn larger and in more detail.'),
           ('Lines of longitude are all the same distance apart everywhere on Earth.', 'Lines of longitude converge at the poles: 1° of longitude is about 111 km at the equator but 0 km at the poles. Lines of latitude are (almost) evenly spaced.'),
           ('A bearing of 090° means facing north.', 'Bearings are measured clockwise from north (000°), so 090° is due east.')],
     cases=[('the Ordnance Survey 1:25 000 Explorer map of the Lake District', 'Walkers use contours, grid references and the scale bar to plan routes over fells such as Scafell Pike (978 m), England\'s highest mountain.', 'the practical use of map skills', 'contours show slope and height, grid references locate features and scale converts route length into walking time.'),
            ('the International Meridian Conference of 1884 in Washington, D.C.', 'Delegates from 25 nations voted to adopt Greenwich as the prime meridian (0°) for the world.', 'why map conventions are agreed internationally', 'a single reference line for longitude allowed consistent navigation charts and, later, standard time zones.')],
     evaluate=[('Digital maps on phones have made traditional map-reading skills unnecessary.', ['GPS gives instant location and turn-by-turn directions', 'digital maps update quickly and can show live data such as traffic'], ['batteries and signal fail in remote areas and emergencies', 'map skills such as reading contours help people judge terrain and understand space, not just follow a line'], 'Largely false: digital tools are powerful but depend on power and signal, and map-reading builds spatial understanding that users need to interpret and check digital maps.')],
     src=[('Official report (paraphrased)', 'The delegates resolved that a single prime meridian should be adopted for all nations in place of the many then in use, and that it should pass through the centre of the transit instrument at the Observatory of Greenwich. France abstained and continued to use the Paris meridian on its own maps for some decades.', 'Summary of the resolutions of the International Meridian Conference, Washington, D.C., October 1884', 'to record the decision of an official international conference', 'It is an official record of the decision, showing the date and that agreement was not unanimous.', 'It records the decision but not the debates or the reasons why individual countries voted as they did.', 'A single world prime meridian at Greenwich was agreed in 1884, although not every country accepted it at once.')],
     rq=[('How accurately do students at our school estimate walking distances compared with measurements from a 1:25 000 map?', 'What are maps?', 'it is a closed, very broad definition question with no place, group or measurable focus', 'It practises scale and distance skills, links to the related concept of scale and produces data the student can collect first-hand.', 'Choose a route; measure it on a 1:25 000 map using string and the scale bar; survey 20 students for estimates; walk the route with a GPS app; compare results in a table and graph; evaluate measurement errors.')])
