K.st('ch04-st1', 'chronology, BCE/CE dating and timelines',
     terms=[('chronology', 'the arrangement of events in the order in which they happened'),
            ('BCE', '"Before the Common Era": years counted backwards before the traditional birth year of Jesus, equivalent to BC'),
            ('CE', '"Common Era": years counted forwards from year 1, equivalent to AD'),
            ('century', 'a period of 100 years; the 15th century CE runs from 1401 to 1500'),
            ('millennium', 'a period of 1 000 years'),
            ('anachronism', 'something placed in the wrong historical period, such as a clock in a scene from ancient Rome'),
            ('circa (c.)', 'a Latin word meaning "about", used before an approximate date')],
     facts=[('Which year comes immediately after 1 BCE?', '1 CE', ['0 CE', '0 BCE', '2 BCE'], 'There is no year 0 in the BCE/CE system.'),
            ('In which century is the year 1066?', 'The 11th century CE', ['The 10th century CE', 'The 12th century CE', 'The 1st century CE'], '1001–1100 is the 11th century.'),
            ('Which of these dates is the earliest?', '2560 BCE', ['753 BCE', '44 BCE', '1066 CE'], 'Larger BCE numbers are further back in time.'),
            ('Why do many historians now use BCE/CE instead of BC/AD?', 'They are religiously neutral terms for the same dating system', ['They count years from a different starting point', 'They were introduced by the Romans', 'They make dates more precise'], 'The year numbers are identical.')],
     why=[('Explain why dates BCE get smaller as time moves forward.', 'BCE years are counted backwards from the start of the Common Era, so an event in 300 BCE is earlier than one in 200 BCE, and the numbers decrease towards 1 BCE.'),
          ('Explain why historians often give dates for ancient events with "c." in front of them.', 'Ancient records are incomplete and methods such as radiocarbon dating give ranges, so historians show the date is an estimate.'),
          ('Explain why a timeline needs a consistent scale.', 'If equal distances do not represent equal amounts of time, the timeline gives a misleading impression of how close or far apart events were.'),
          ('Explain why different societies have used different calendars.', 'Calendars are based on different starting events and astronomical cycles – e.g. the Islamic Hijri calendar counts from 622 CE and is lunar, while the Chinese calendar is lunisolar.')],
     contrast=[('chronology', 'causation', 'Chronology is the order in which events happened; causation is why they happened and how one event led to another – an earlier event is not automatically a cause.'),
               ('a primary date', 'an approximate date', 'A primary date is recorded at the time (e.g. on a dated document); an approximate date (c.) is estimated by historians or archaeologists from evidence.')],
     misc=[('The year 1500 is in the 16th century.', '1500 is the last year of the 15th century (1401–1500); the 16th century begins in 1501.'),
           ('From 50 BCE to 50 CE is 100 years.', 'Because there is no year 0, it is 99 years.'),
           ('If one event comes before another, the first must have caused the second.', 'Sequence is not cause: historians need evidence of a link before claiming causation.')],
     cases=[('the Hijri calendar', 'It counts years from the Hijra, Muhammad\'s migration from Mecca to Medina in 622 CE, and uses 12 lunar months.', 'different chronologies', 'societies anchor time to events important to them, so historians convert between calendars.'),
            ('radiocarbon dating of the Indus city of Mohenjo-daro', 'Dates from organic remains place its mature phase at about 2500–1900 BCE.', 'how historians date the distant past', 'scientific methods give approximate dates, so ancient chronology is written with "c.".')],
     evaluate=[('Learning dates is the most important skill in history.', ['chronology lets historians see sequence, change and continuity', 'without dates causation cannot be judged'], ['interpreting evidence and explaining causes matter more than memorising dates', 'many dates are approximate'], 'Disagree: secure chronology is a foundation, but the key historical skills are explaining, interpreting and evaluating – dates serve those skills.')],
     src=[('Ancient history (paraphrased)', 'The Greek writer described Egypt as a land made by the river: each year the Nile flooded the fields, and when the waters went down the Egyptians sowed their crops in the rich mud it left behind.', 'Paraphrase of Herodotus, "The Histories", Book II, written c. 430 BCE', 'to describe Egypt and its customs for a Greek audience', 'An early outsider\'s description, written more than 2 000 years after the pyramids were built, that shows how ancient writers understood the Nile.', 'Herodotus visited Egypt centuries after the Old Kingdom and relied partly on what priests told him, so his chronology is not reliable.', 'Egyptian farming and civilisation depended on the annual Nile flood.')],
     rq=[('How did the Egyptian calendar of three seasons reflect the annual flooding of the Nile?', 'When did history begin?', 'it is too broad and philosophical to answer with sources', 'It links chronology and time-keeping to geography and has accessible primary and secondary evidence.', 'Read museum websites (British Museum, Met) and a textbook; record the three seasons (Akhet, Peret, Shemu) and their months; compare with Nile flood data; create a timeline; cite sources in MLA.')])
C.chronology(K, 'ch04-st1')

K.st('ch04-st2', 'river valley civilisations of the Indus, Mesopotamia, Egypt and China',
     terms=[('civilisation', 'a complex society with cities, organised government, specialised jobs, social classes, writing and monumental buildings'),
            ('cuneiform', 'the wedge-shaped writing pressed into clay tablets in Mesopotamia from about 3200 BCE'),
            ('hieroglyphs', 'the writing system of ancient Egypt that used pictures and symbols'),
            ('ziggurat', 'a stepped temple tower built in Mesopotamian cities such as Ur'),
            ('irrigation', 'supplying water to crops through canals and channels'),
            ('surplus', 'more food than a farming community needs to survive, allowing some people to do other jobs'),
            ('oracle bones', 'animal bones and shells inscribed by Shang dynasty diviners, giving the earliest Chinese writing')],
     facts=[('Mohenjo-daro and Harappa were cities of which civilisation?', 'The Indus Valley (Harappan) civilisation', ['Mesopotamia', 'Ancient Egypt', 'The Shang dynasty'], 'Both lie in present-day Pakistan.'),
            ('Mesopotamia lay between which two rivers?', 'The Tigris and the Euphrates', ['The Nile and the Congo', 'The Indus and the Ganges', 'The Huang He and the Yangtze'], 'Mesopotamia means "land between the rivers".'),
            ('Which ruler of Babylon issued a famous law code carved on a stone stele in about 1754 BCE?', 'Hammurabi', ['Tutankhamun', 'Ashoka', 'Qin Shi Huang'], 'The stele is in the Louvre.'),
            ('Which feature is especially associated with Indus cities?', 'Grid-pattern streets and covered drains', ['Pyramids', 'Ziggurats', 'The Great Wall'], 'Mohenjo-daro also had the Great Bath.'),
            ('Which river valley was home to China\'s Shang dynasty?', 'The Huang He (Yellow River)', ['The Mekong', 'The Indus', 'The Amur'], 'The Shang ruled c. 1600–1046 BCE.')],
     why=[('Explain why early civilisations developed in river valleys.', 'Rivers gave water for drinking and irrigation, fertile silt after floods, fish and transport; farming produced a surplus that supported cities, rulers and specialised workers.'),
          ('Explain why writing developed in early civilisations such as Mesopotamia.', 'Temples and rulers needed to record taxes, trade, stores of grain and laws; cuneiform began as tallies and pictographs for accounts.'),
          ('Explain why historians still know less about the Indus civilisation than about Egypt.', 'The Indus script has not been deciphered, so we cannot read their own records; knowledge comes mostly from archaeology.'),
          ('Explain why controlling floods and irrigation could increase the power of rulers.', 'Large canal and dyke systems needed organised labour and planning, so leaders who organised them could demand taxes and loyalty.')],
     contrast=[('the Egyptian civilisation', 'the Indus Valley civilisation', 'Egypt had powerful god-kings (pharaohs), monumental tombs and readable hieroglyphs; the Indus cities show careful planning and drains but no grand palaces or royal tombs, and their script is undeciphered.'),
               ('a city-state', 'an empire', 'A city-state is one city and its surrounding land with its own ruler (e.g. Ur); an empire is a large area including many peoples ruled by one power (e.g. Akkad under Sargon).')],
     misc=[('Ancient Egypt depended on regular rain for farming.', 'Egypt receives very little rain; farming depended on the annual Nile flood and irrigation.'),
           ('The pyramids were built by enslaved prisoners of war.', 'Evidence from workers\' villages at Giza suggests organised, paid and fed labour crews, including seasonal farmers; slavery existed in Egypt but did not build the pyramids in the way often shown in films.'),
           ('We can read the Indus script, so we know the names of its kings.', 'The Indus script is undeciphered; we do not know the names of any rulers.')],
     cases=[('the Great Bath at Mohenjo-daro', 'A large watertight brick pool, made with gypsum mortar and bitumen, at the heart of the citadel (c. 2500 BCE).', 'the technology and organisation of Indus cities', 'it shows skilled building and possibly religious ritual, and the planning needed to supply and drain water.'),
            ('the Code of Hammurabi', 'Nearly 300 laws carved on a basalt stele set out punishments that varied by social class.', 'law and social hierarchy in Mesopotamia', 'it shows a ruler using written law to keep order and claim justice, and that society was divided into classes.')],
     evaluate=[('Rivers were the single most important reason why the first civilisations developed.', ['all four early civilisations grew in river valleys', 'rivers gave water, silt and transport needed for a food surplus'], ['organisation, leadership and religion turned farming villages into cities', 'trade and technology such as writing and bronze were also essential'], 'Rivers were a necessary condition, but not sufficient: civilisation also needed social organisation, technology and trade.')],
     src=[('Law code (paraphrased)', 'If a free man destroys the eye of another free man, his own eye shall be destroyed. If he destroys the eye of a commoner, he shall pay one mina of silver. If he destroys the eye of a man\'s slave, he shall pay half the slave\'s value.', 'Paraphrase of laws 196, 198 and 199 of the Code of Hammurabi, Babylon, c. 1754 BCE (stele now in the Louvre, Paris)', 'to set out the king\'s laws and show Hammurabi as a just ruler chosen by the gods', 'It is an original primary source from the ruler himself and reveals the social classes of Babylon.', 'It shows the laws as written, not how they were applied in real courts; it presents an idealised royal image.', 'Punishments in Babylon depended on the social class of the victim.')],
     rq=[('What does the town planning of Mohenjo-daro suggest about how the Indus civilisation was organised?', 'What was life like in the past?', 'it is far too broad: no civilisation, time or aspect is named', 'It uses archaeological evidence to investigate an open historical question about an undeciphered civilisation.', 'Study site plans and photographs from the Harappa.com archive and museum websites; read two scholarly summaries; record evidence on streets, drains and buildings in a table; compare interpretations; evaluate the limits of archaeology alone.')])
C.chronology(K, 'ch04-st2', c='C3', d=(2, 3), events=[('the first cuneiform tablets at Uruk (approx.)', -3200), ('the building of the Great Pyramid of Giza (approx.)', -2560), ('the height of Mohenjo-daro (approx.)', -2500), ('Hammurabi\'s law code (approx.)', -1754), ('the start of the Shang dynasty (approx.)', -1600), ('the end of the Shang dynasty', -1046), ('the decline of the Indus cities (approx.)', -1900), ('the reign of Tutankhamun begins (approx.)', -1332)])

K.st('ch04-st3', 'the Silk Road: its trade routes, goods, ideas and religions',
     terms=[('Silk Road', 'the network of overland and sea trade routes linking China with Central Asia, India, the Middle East and Europe from about the 2nd century BCE'),
            ('caravanserai', 'a roadside inn with a courtyard where merchants and their animals rested on Silk Road routes'),
            ('merchant', 'a person who buys and sells goods to make a profit'),
            ('cultural diffusion', 'the spread of ideas, beliefs, technologies and customs from one society to another'),
            ('oasis town', 'a settlement around a water source in a desert, such as Dunhuang or Kashgar, that served as a stop on trade routes'),
            ('Pax Mongolica', 'the period of relative peace across the Mongol Empire in the 13th and 14th centuries that made long-distance trade safer')],
     facts=[('Which Han dynasty envoy\'s missions to Central Asia from about 138 BCE helped open the Silk Road?', 'Zhang Qian', ['Marco Polo', 'Ibn Battuta', 'Zheng He'], 'He reported on the horses of Ferghana and western kingdoms.'),
            ('Who first used the name "Silk Road" (Seidenstraße) in 1877?', 'Ferdinand von Richthofen', ['Marco Polo', 'Genghis Khan', 'Herodotus'], 'He was a German geographer.'),
            ('Which religion spread from India along the Silk Road to China?', 'Buddhism', ['Christianity first', 'Shinto', 'Sikhism'], 'Buddhist monasteries and cave temples such as Mogao at Dunhuang lie on the routes.'),
            ('Which city in present-day Uzbekistan was a major Silk Road trading centre?', 'Samarkand', ['Kyoto', 'Lisbon', 'Timbuktu'], 'It was rebuilt as Timur\'s capital in the 14th century.'),
            ('Which disease is believed to have spread west along trade routes in the 1340s?', 'The Black Death (plague)', ['Smallpox', 'Influenza in 1918', 'Cholera'], 'It reached the Black Sea and then Europe in 1347.')],
     why=[('Explain why few merchants travelled the whole length of the Silk Road.', 'Distances were huge and dangerous, so goods passed through many hands; merchants traded over sections between oasis towns and middlemen made a profit at each stage.'),
          ('Explain why silk was so valuable in Rome.', 'Only China knew how to produce silk, and it travelled thousands of kilometres, so it was rare and expensive and became a symbol of wealth and status.'),
          ('Explain why the Mongol Empire boosted trade along the Silk Road.', 'One empire controlled most of the route, so merchants faced fewer borders, safer roads and a relay postal system (yam), which lowered risk and cost.'),
          ('Explain why the Silk Road was as important for ideas as for goods.', 'Travellers carried religions, languages, art styles, scientific knowledge and technologies such as papermaking, which changed societies along the way.')],
     contrast=[('the overland Silk Road', 'the maritime Silk Road', 'The overland routes crossed deserts and mountains by camel caravan; the maritime routes used monsoon winds to sail between China, South-east Asia, India, Arabia and East Africa and could carry bulkier goods.'),
               ('trade in goods', 'cultural diffusion', 'Trade in goods is the exchange of products such as silk and spices; cultural diffusion is the spread of beliefs, ideas and technologies that often travelled with that trade.')],
     misc=[('The Silk Road was a single paved road from China to Rome.', 'It was a changing network of land and sea routes; most travellers covered only part of it.'),
           ('Only silk was traded along the Silk Road.', 'Spices, horses, glass, gold, silver, jade, porcelain, paper and many other goods were traded, along with ideas.'),
           ('The Silk Road only helped Europe.', 'Central Asian cities, Persia, India and China all grew rich from the trade and exchanged ideas in all directions.')],
     cases=[('the Mogao Caves at Dunhuang', 'Hundreds of Buddhist cave temples were cut and painted between the 4th and 14th centuries at this oasis town; a hidden library held thousands of manuscripts in many languages.', 'cultural exchange on the Silk Road', 'Indian, Central Asian and Chinese art styles and texts met at one oasis on the route.'),
            ('Marco Polo\'s journey to the court of Kublai Khan (c. 1275)', 'The Venetian merchant described Mongol China, paper money and great cities in a book that circulated widely in Europe.', 'the role of the Pax Mongolica', 'Mongol control made such long journeys possible and spread knowledge of Asia to Europe.')],
     evaluate=[('The exchange of ideas along the Silk Road was more important than the exchange of goods.', ['religions such as Buddhism and Islam spread and lasted for centuries', 'technologies like paper changed learning and government'], ['trade in goods made cities rich and paid for the journeys that carried ideas', 'disease also spread, causing the Black Death'], 'Ideas had the longest-lasting impact, but they travelled because of the trade in goods – the two cannot really be separated.')],
     src=[('Travel account (paraphrased)', 'The traveller described the Great Khan\'s paper money, made from the bark of mulberry trees and stamped with the ruler\'s seal. Everyone in the empire had to accept it in payment, and merchants exchanged their gold, pearls and precious stones for it.', 'Paraphrase of "The Travels of Marco Polo", written down by Rustichello da Pisa c. 1298', 'to describe the wonders of the East for European readers and entertain them', 'It is one of the few European eyewitness accounts of Mongol China and describes a real Yuan institution, paper money.', 'It was written years later with a co-author who wrote romances, so some details may be exaggerated; some historians question parts of it.', 'Mongol China used paper money that everyone had to accept in trade.')],
     rq=[('How did the spread of Buddhism along the Silk Road influence art at the Mogao Caves between 400 and 1000 CE?', 'What was traded on the Silk Road?', 'it is a simple recall question that could be answered with a list', 'It investigates cultural diffusion, a key concept of the unit, using visual primary sources that are available online.', 'Use the Dunhuang Foundation digital archive and two secondary sources; choose five cave paintings; record features (style, dress, figures) in a table; compare with Indian and Chinese art; cite sources in MLA; evaluate dating evidence.')])
C.chronology(K, 'ch04-st3', c='C3', d=(2, 3), events=[('Zhang Qian\'s first mission west (approx.)', -138), ('the Han dynasty founded', -202), ('the Tang capital Chang\'an at its height (approx.)', 750), ('Genghis Khan takes his title', 1206), ('Marco Polo reaches Kublai Khan\'s court (approx.)', 1275), ('the Black Death reaches the Black Sea', 1347), ('Ibn Battuta reaches China (approx.)', 1345), ('Richthofen names the "Silk Road"', 1877)])

K.st('ch04-st4', 'cultural exchange and the spread of technologies such as paper, gunpowder and numerals',
     terms=[('Hindu–Arabic numerals', 'the ten-digit place-value number system with a zero, developed in India and spread by Arab and Persian scholars'),
            ('place value', 'a number system in which the value of a digit depends on its position, such as tens and hundreds'),
            ('woodblock printing', 'printing by carving a whole page in reverse on a wooden block, inking it and pressing paper onto it'),
            ('gunpowder', 'an explosive mixture of saltpetre, charcoal and sulphur first made in China'),
            ('technology transfer', 'the movement of tools, techniques and knowledge from one society to another')],
     facts=[('According to Chinese records, who improved papermaking in 105 CE?', 'Cai Lun', ['Gutenberg', 'Zhang Qian', 'Confucius'], 'He was a court official of the Han dynasty.'),
            ('What is the earliest dated printed book that survives, printed in 868 CE and found at Dunhuang?', 'The Diamond Sutra', ['The Gutenberg Bible', 'The Quran of Uthman', 'The Book of Kells'], 'It is now in the British Library.'),
            ('Which Indian mathematician wrote rules for calculating with zero in 628 CE?', 'Brahmagupta', ['Euclid', 'Fibonacci', 'al-Khwarizmi'], 'His work was later translated into Arabic.'),
            ('Which Italian\'s book "Liber Abaci" (1202) helped spread Hindu–Arabic numerals in Europe?', 'Fibonacci (Leonardo of Pisa)', ['Galileo', 'Marco Polo', 'Leonardo da Vinci'], 'He learned them in North Africa.'),
            ('In which city was an early paper mill established in about 794 CE, spreading paper across the Islamic world?', 'Baghdad', ['Paris', 'Rome', 'Delhi'], 'Abbasid Baghdad was a great centre of learning.')],
     why=[('Explain why paper spread rapidly through the Islamic world after the 8th century.', 'Paper was cheaper and lighter than parchment or papyrus, and the Abbasid state, scholars and merchants needed large amounts for records, books and translation in centres such as Baghdad.'),
          ('Explain why the Hindu–Arabic numeral system replaced Roman numerals for calculation in Europe.', 'Place value and zero made addition, multiplication and accounting far easier; merchants and bankers adopted it, although some cities at first banned it in official records for fear of fraud.'),
          ('Explain why the spread of gunpowder changed warfare.', 'Cannons could break castle and city walls and handguns reduced the advantage of armoured knights, helping larger states with money for artillery to grow – such as the Ottoman, Safavid and Mughal empires.'),
          ('Explain why technologies often took centuries to spread from China to Europe.', 'They travelled slowly through many intermediaries along trade routes; some, like papermaking, were kept secret, and each society had to adapt them to local needs and materials.')],
     contrast=[('invention', 'diffusion', 'Invention is the creation of a new technology in one place; diffusion is its spread from that place to other societies.'),
               ('woodblock printing', 'movable-type printing', 'Woodblock printing carves a whole page into one block; movable type uses separate reusable characters that can be rearranged – first in China (Bi Sheng, 1040s), Korea (metal type) and later Europe (Gutenberg, c. 1450).')],
     misc=[('Arabic numerals were invented by Arabs.', 'They were developed in India; Arab and Persian scholars such as al-Khwarizmi spread them, which is why Europeans called them "Arabic".'),
           ('Gutenberg invented printing.', 'Printing existed in China for centuries before; Gutenberg developed a movable metal-type press in Europe around 1450.'),
           ('Gunpowder was first invented in Europe for cannons.', 'It was developed in China, where a formula appears in a military text of 1044, and spread west by the 13th century.')],
     cases=[('the Battle of Talas (751 CE)', 'Abbasid forces defeated a Tang army in Central Asia; a later tradition says captured Chinese papermakers taught the craft in Samarkand.', 'the transfer of technology through contact', 'even conflict along trade routes could move skills from one civilisation to another.'),
            ('the House of Wisdom in Baghdad', 'Scholars in the 9th century translated Greek, Persian and Indian texts into Arabic; al-Khwarizmi wrote on Indian numerals and algebra.', 'cultural exchange of knowledge', 'collecting and translating knowledge from many cultures created new mathematics that later reached Europe.')],
     evaluate=[('Paper was the most important technology to travel along the Silk Road.', ['it made books and records cheaper and spread learning and bureaucracy', 'it later enabled the printing revolution'], ['gunpowder transformed warfare and the power of states', 'numerals changed trade, science and mathematics'], 'A strong case, because paper underpinned learning, government and printing, but gunpowder and numerals had equally deep effects in other areas.')],
     src=[('Mathematical text (paraphrased)', 'The author explained that with the nine Indian figures and the sign 0, which the Arabs call zephirum, any number at all can be written. He set out how merchants could use the method for calculating prices, weights and exchanges of money.', 'Paraphrase of the opening of Leonardo of Pisa (Fibonacci), "Liber Abaci", 1202', 'to teach Italian merchants the Hindu–Arabic numeral system and its practical uses', 'It shows when and why the numerals were introduced to Europe and credits their Indian origin.', 'It reflects one author\'s view; it does not show how quickly ordinary people actually adopted the system.', 'Indian numerals with zero can write any number and are useful for trade.')],
     rq=[('How did the arrival of paper change the way books were produced in Baghdad between 750 and 900 CE?', 'Who invented everything?', 'it is far too broad and based on the false idea that one person invents everything', 'It focuses on one technology, one city and a clear period, so evidence can be found.', 'Read two scholarly articles and a museum resource on Abbasid manuscripts; take Cornell notes; build a timeline; compare costs of papyrus, parchment and paper; cite sources; evaluate gaps in evidence.')])
C.chronology(K, 'ch04-st4', c='C3', d=(2, 4), events=[('Cai Lun improves papermaking', 105), ('Brahmagupta writes rules for zero', 628), ('the Battle of Talas', 751), ('a paper mill in Baghdad (approx.)', 794), ('the Diamond Sutra is printed', 868), ('a gunpowder formula appears in the Wujing Zongyao', 1044), ('Fibonacci publishes Liber Abaci', 1202), ('Gutenberg prints his Bible (approx.)', 1455)])

# =============================================================================================
# ch05 Medieval Societies and Power
# =============================================================================================
K.st('ch05-st1', 'feudal society in medieval Europe',
     terms=[('feudalism', 'a medieval system in which land was granted in return for loyalty and service, linking the king, nobles, knights and peasants'),
            ('vassal', 'a person who received land (a fief) from a lord in return for loyalty and military service'),
            ('serf', 'a peasant tied to a lord\'s land who could not leave without permission and had to work for the lord'),
            ('manor', 'an estate held by a lord, including his house, farmland and a village of peasants'),
            ('tithe', 'one-tenth of a person\'s produce or income paid to the Church'),
            ('Magna Carta', 'the charter agreed by King John and his barons in 1215 that limited the king\'s power and promised justice under the law')],
     facts=[('Which survey of land and property in England did William I order in 1085–86?', 'The Domesday Book', ['Magna Carta', 'The Bayeux Tapestry', 'The Doomsday Act'], 'It recorded who held land and what it was worth.'),
            ('In which year was Magna Carta sealed at Runnymede?', '1215', ['1066', '1348', '1381'], 'King John agreed it with rebellious barons.'),
            ('About what share of Europe\'s population died in the Black Death of 1347–1351?', 'Between a third and a half', ['About 5%', 'About 90%', 'Under 1%'], 'Estimates vary by region.'),
            ('Who were at the top of the feudal system under the king?', 'Barons and bishops', ['Serfs', 'Merchants', 'Knights only'], 'They held land directly from the king.'),
            ('Which 1381 uprising in England was partly a protest against the poll tax?', 'The Peasants\' Revolt', ['The Glorious Revolution', 'The Pilgrimage of Grace', 'The Jacquerie'], 'It was led by Wat Tyler.')],
     why=[('Explain why William I introduced a feudal system after conquering England in 1066.', 'He needed to control a hostile population with a small army, so he granted land to loyal Norman nobles who built castles and provided knights, binding them to him by oaths.'),
          ('Explain why the Church was so powerful in medieval Europe.', 'It owned large amounts of land, collected tithes, controlled education and literacy, and people believed it held the key to salvation, so even kings needed its support.'),
          ('Explain why the Black Death weakened the feudal system.', 'So many peasants died that labour became scarce; survivors demanded higher wages or left their manors, and attempts to fix wages led to unrest such as the Peasants\' Revolt.'),
          ('Explain why Magna Carta is seen as important today.', 'It established the idea that the ruler is subject to the law and that free men could not be imprisoned without lawful judgement, an idea later used in constitutional and human-rights documents.')],
     contrast=[('a lord', 'a serf', 'A lord held land and had rights over the people on his manor; a serf worked the lord\'s land, owed him labour and could not leave without permission.'),
               ('a knight', 'a baron', 'A baron was a great noble who held large estates directly from the king; a knight was a mounted warrior who usually held a smaller fief from a baron in return for military service.')],
     misc=[('Feudalism worked exactly the same way everywhere in medieval Europe.', '"Feudalism" is a modern label; arrangements of land and service varied greatly between countries and centuries.'),
           ('Magna Carta gave rights to all English people in 1215.', 'Most clauses protected barons and free men; serfs, the majority, gained little at the time.'),
           ('Medieval peasants never resisted their lords.', 'Peasants resisted through petitions, poaching and revolts such as the 1381 Peasants\' Revolt and the French Jacquerie of 1358.')],
     cases=[('the Domesday Book (1086)', 'Royal officials recorded land, people, animals and values in over 13 000 places in England.', 'royal power in a feudal society', 'the king could see what every lord held and taxed, strengthening central control.'),
            ('the Peasants\' Revolt of 1381', 'After a third poll tax, rebels from Essex and Kent marched on London; leader Wat Tyler was killed and promises made by Richard II were broken.', 'challenges to the feudal order', 'labour shortages after the Black Death gave peasants confidence to demand freedom from serfdom.')],
     evaluate=[('The Black Death was the main reason for the decline of feudalism in England.', ['labour shortages raised wages and peasants\' bargaining power', 'it led to the Statute of Labourers and the 1381 revolt'], ['money rents were already replacing labour services before 1348', 'the growth of towns, trade and royal government also weakened feudal ties'], 'An important accelerator rather than the only cause: it sped up changes in labour and money rents that had already begun.')],
     src=[('Royal charter (paraphrased)', 'No free man shall be seized or imprisoned, or stripped of his rights or possessions, or outlawed or exiled, except by the lawful judgement of his equals or by the law of the land. To no one will we sell, deny or delay right or justice.', 'Paraphrase of clauses 39 and 40 of Magna Carta, sealed by King John at Runnymede, June 1215', 'to make peace between the king and rebel barons by promising to rule according to law', 'It is the original agreement and shows the limits the barons wanted to place on royal power.', 'It was a peace treaty that King John soon rejected, and it protected "free men", not the unfree majority.', 'The king must not punish free men without lawful judgement and must not sell or delay justice.')],
     rq=[('How did labour shortages after the Black Death change the lives of peasants in England between 1348 and 1400?', 'Was the Middle Ages bad?', 'it is a vague value judgement covering a thousand years', 'It investigates change and causation with a clear period and place, using manor records and secondary studies.', 'Read two secondary accounts; examine translated sources (Statute of Labourers 1351, manor court rolls); record evidence about wages and services in a table; build a timeline; evaluate how typical the sources are.')])
C.chronology(K, 'ch05-st1', c='C3', d=(1, 2), events=[('the Battle of Hastings', 1066), ('the Domesday survey is completed', 1086), ('Magna Carta is sealed', 1215), ('the first Parliament with knights and burgesses (de Montfort\'s)', 1265), ('the Black Death reaches England', 1348), ('the Statute of Labourers', 1351), ('the Peasants\' Revolt', 1381), ('the coronation of Charlemagne', 800)])

K.st('ch05-st2', 'the Mughal Empire: administration, culture and religion',
     terms=[('Mughal Empire', 'the empire founded by Babur in 1526 that ruled much of the Indian subcontinent until the 18th century'),
            ('mansabdari system', 'Akbar\'s system of ranks (mansabs) in which officials held a rank that fixed their pay and the number of cavalry they had to supply'),
            ('jizya', 'a tax on non-Muslim subjects in some Islamic states, abolished by Akbar in 1564 and restored by Aurangzeb in 1679'),
            ('sulh-i kul', '"peace with all": Akbar\'s policy of tolerance towards people of all religions'),
            ('zabt', 'the land revenue system developed by Raja Todar Mal that assessed tax on measured land and average crop yields'),
            ('Akbarnama', 'the official history of Akbar\'s reign written by his court historian Abul Fazl')],
     facts=[('Which battle in 1526 allowed Babur to found the Mughal Empire?', 'The First Battle of Panipat', ['The Battle of Plassey', 'The Battle of Talikota', 'The Battle of Haldighati'], 'Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi, Sultan of Delhi.'),
            ('Which emperor built the Taj Mahal in memory of his wife Mumtaz Mahal?', 'Shah Jahan', ['Akbar', 'Babur', 'Aurangzeb'], 'Construction ran c. 1632–1653 in Agra.'),
            ('Which language was used in the Mughal court and administration?', 'Persian', ['English', 'Sanskrit', 'Portuguese'], 'Persian was the language of government and high culture.'),
            ('Which Mughal emperor founded the city of Fatehpur Sikri?', 'Akbar', ['Humayun', 'Jahangir', 'Aurangzeb'], 'It was built in the 1570s.'),
            ('Which emperor ruled from 1658 to 1707, when the empire reached its greatest extent?', 'Aurangzeb', ['Akbar', 'Shah Jahan', 'Bahadur Shah Zafar'], 'Long wars in the Deccan strained the empire.')],
     why=[('Explain why Akbar followed a policy of religious tolerance.', 'Most of his subjects were Hindu; tolerance (abolishing jizya, marriage alliances with Rajput families, debates at the Ibadat Khana) won loyalty, reduced revolts and drew talented officials of all faiths into government.'),
          ('Explain why the mansabdari system strengthened the emperor\'s control.', 'Every officer\'s rank, pay and troop numbers were set by the emperor, and postings were transferable, so nobles depended on him rather than on hereditary local power.'),
          ('Explain why the Mughal Empire declined after 1707.', 'Long and costly wars, especially in the Deccan, weak successors, revolts by Marathas, Sikhs and Jats, and governors becoming independent weakened central power; foreign invasions (Nadir Shah, 1739) and European companies took advantage.'),
          ('Explain why Mughal architecture combined Persian and Indian styles.', 'The dynasty came from Central Asia with Persian culture but employed Indian craftsmen and ruled Indian subjects, so buildings like Fatehpur Sikri mixed domes and gardens with local stone, chhatris and decoration.')],
     contrast=[('Akbar\'s religious policy', 'Aurangzeb\'s religious policy', 'Akbar promoted tolerance (sulh-i kul), abolished the jizya and employed many Hindus; Aurangzeb ruled more strictly according to his interpretation of Islamic law and reimposed the jizya in 1679, although he also employed Hindu nobles.'),
               ('a mansabdar', 'a zamindar', 'A mansabdar was an imperial officer with a rank and salary set by the emperor; a zamindar was a local landholder with hereditary rights to collect revenue from peasants.')],
     misc=[('The Mughals ruled only through force.', 'They also used alliances (e.g. with Rajput rulers), a salaried officer corps, revenue systems and patronage of art and religion to win loyalty.'),
           ('Akbar created a new religion that all his subjects had to follow.', 'Din-i Ilahi was a small spiritual circle of courtiers; it was never imposed on the population.'),
           ('The Mughal Empire was poor compared with Europe.', 'Around 1700 Mughal India was one of the world\'s largest economies, famed for textiles, and its revenues dwarfed those of most European states.')],
     cases=[('Raja Todar Mal\'s land revenue reforms under Akbar', 'Land was measured and classified, and tax was set as a share of average yields over ten years, paid mostly in cash.', 'efficient administration', 'a fair and predictable tax increased revenue and reduced peasant hardship and corruption.'),
            ('the Ibadat Khana at Fatehpur Sikri', 'From 1575 Akbar held debates there between Muslim, Hindu, Jain, Zoroastrian and Christian scholars.', 'religion and power under the Mughals', 'Akbar used dialogue between faiths to develop a policy of tolerance that strengthened his rule.')],
     evaluate=[('Akbar was the most successful Mughal emperor.', ['he expanded the empire and built lasting systems (mansabdari, zabt)', 'his tolerance won the loyalty of diverse subjects'], ['Aurangzeb ruled the largest territory', 'Shah Jahan\'s reign was a high point of wealth and architecture'], 'Akbar has the strongest claim because his administrative and religious policies created stability that lasted for a century, even if the empire was larger later.')],
     src=[('Court history (paraphrased)', 'The historian praised the emperor as a wise ruler who treated people of every faith with kindness, listened to the arguments of learned men of all religions and placed justice above everything, so that the land was peaceful and prosperous.', 'Paraphrase of Abul Fazl, "Akbarnama", written at Akbar\'s court, c. 1590s', 'to record and glorify the reign of Akbar for the emperor and his court', 'It is detailed, written by someone close to the emperor with access to court records, and explains the ideas behind Akbar\'s policies.', 'Abul Fazl was Akbar\'s friend and official historian, so it presents an idealised picture and omits criticism.', 'Akbar was a tolerant, just ruler who brought peace and prosperity.')],
     rq=[('How did Akbar use marriage alliances and appointments to win the loyalty of Rajput rulers between 1562 and 1605?', 'Who were the Mughals?', 'it is descriptive and too broad, so it would produce a summary rather than an investigation', 'It focuses on a clear method of power and a defined period, with primary (Akbarnama, Ain-i-Akbari) and secondary sources available.', 'Read translated extracts from the Akbarnama; two secondary sources; list Rajput nobles and their ranks; build a timeline; compare perspectives (Mughal court vs Rajput chronicles); cite sources; evaluate bias.')])
C.chronology(K, 'ch05-st2', c='C3', d=(1, 2), events=[('the First Battle of Panipat', 1526), ('Akbar becomes emperor', 1556), ('Akbar abolishes the jizya', 1564), ('Fatehpur Sikri is founded (approx.)', 1571), ('work begins on the Taj Mahal (approx.)', 1632), ('Aurangzeb becomes emperor', 1658), ('Aurangzeb reimposes the jizya', 1679), ('the death of Aurangzeb', 1707), ('Nadir Shah sacks Delhi', 1739)])

K.st('ch05-st3', 'primary and secondary sources of evidence in history',
     terms=[('primary source', 'a source created at the time of the event or by someone who experienced it'),
            ('secondary source', 'a source created later by someone who did not witness the event, usually interpreting primary sources'),
            ('origin', 'who created a source, when and where'),
            ('purpose', 'why a source was created and for which audience'),
            ('bias', 'a one-sided view that presents information in a way that favours a particular side'),
            ('corroboration', 'checking a source against other sources to see whether they agree'),
            ('provenance', 'the history of a source: where it came from and how it has been kept')],
     facts=[('Which of these is a primary source for the Norman Conquest of 1066?', 'The Bayeux Tapestry', ['A 2020 documentary', 'A modern textbook', 'A historian\'s biography of William I'], 'It was made in the 1070s.'),
            ('A modern historian\'s book about the Mughal Empire is usually classed as…', 'a secondary source', ['a primary source', 'an artefact', 'an oral source'], 'It interprets earlier evidence.'),
            ('What is the "purpose" of a source?', 'The reason it was made and the audience it was made for', ['Its date of creation', 'Its physical size', 'The place it is kept today'], 'Purpose helps judge value and limitations.'),
            ('Which question best tests the reliability of a source?', 'Is it supported by other independent sources?', ['Is it long?', 'Is it in colour?', 'Is it famous?'], 'Corroboration tests reliability.'),
            ('Who commissioned the Bayeux Tapestry, most historians believe?', 'Bishop Odo of Bayeux, William\'s half-brother', ['Harold Godwinson', 'King Alfred', 'Pope Gregory'], 'It tells the Norman version of events.')],
     why=[('Explain why a biased source can still be valuable to a historian.', 'It reveals the attitudes and perspective of its creator and audience – e.g. Norman propaganda shows how William justified the conquest – even if it is not a balanced account of events.'),
          ('Explain why historians use more than one source to study an event.', 'Each source has its own perspective and gaps; comparing and corroborating sources gives a fuller and more reliable picture.'),
          ('Explain why a secondary source may sometimes be more useful than a primary source.', 'A historian writing later can use many primary sources, has hindsight and may be more balanced, so for an overview a secondary source can be more useful.'),
          ('Explain why the date of a source matters when judging its value.', 'A source made at the time may record details accurately, while one written long after may rely on memory or others; but a later source may have more information and hindsight.')],
     contrast=[('a primary source', 'a secondary source', 'A primary source comes from the time or from a witness (e.g. a diary, a law code); a secondary source is a later interpretation by someone who was not there (e.g. a textbook).'),
               ('reliability', 'usefulness', 'Reliability is how far a source can be trusted to be accurate; usefulness depends on the question – an unreliable source can still be useful for studying attitudes or propaganda.')],
     misc=[('Primary sources are always more reliable than secondary sources.', 'Primary sources can be biased, mistaken or propaganda; reliability depends on origin, purpose and corroboration, not just type.'),
           ('A biased source is useless.', 'Biased sources are valuable for showing perspectives and how people tried to influence others.'),
           ('If a source is old, it must be true.', 'Age does not guarantee accuracy; ancient writers also made mistakes and wrote for particular purposes.')],
     cases=[('the Bayeux Tapestry', 'A 70 m embroidered cloth made in the 1070s shows the events of 1064–1066 from the Norman point of view, including Harold\'s oath to William.', 'the value and limitations of a primary source', 'it is contemporary and detailed but was commissioned by a Norman bishop to justify the conquest.'),
            ('the Akbarnama', 'Abul Fazl\'s official history of Akbar is a detailed contemporary record, but it praises the emperor throughout.', 'origin and purpose', 'a court historian\'s purpose of glorifying the ruler limits balance but reveals how the Mughals wanted to be seen.')],
     evaluate=[('Historians should rely mainly on primary sources.', ['they are direct evidence from the time', 'they show contemporary perspectives and language'], ['they are often incomplete, biased or hard to interpret without context', 'secondary sources bring together many primary sources and expert analysis'], 'Historians need both: primary sources are the evidence, but secondary sources supply context and debate needed to interpret them.')],
     src=[('Chronicle (paraphrased)', 'The chronicler wrote that Harold had sworn an oath on holy relics to support Duke William\'s claim to the English throne. By taking the crown himself in January 1066, Harold had broken his oath, so William\'s invasion was a just war with the blessing of the Pope.', 'Paraphrase of William of Poitiers, "Gesta Guillelmi" (The Deeds of William), written c. 1071–1077 by William I\'s chaplain', 'to justify the Norman Conquest and praise William', 'Written close to the events by someone at William\'s court, it shows the Norman justification for the invasion.', 'As William\'s chaplain he had a clear reason to present William as rightful and Harold as an oath-breaker; English sources tell a different story.', 'William\'s invasion was justified because Harold broke a sacred oath.')],
     rq=[('How far does the Bayeux Tapestry give a reliable account of the events of 1066?', 'What happened in 1066?', 'it asks only for a narrative and does not require evaluating evidence', 'It practises criterion D source evaluation using a famous, accessible primary source and corroborating chronicles.', 'Study the tapestry online (Bayeux Museum); read the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle and William of Poitiers extracts; compare events in a table; apply origin, purpose, value and limitation; write conclusions with citations.')])

K.st('ch05-st4', 'continuity and change in power across medieval empires',
     terms=[('continuity', 'aspects of life that stay the same over a period of time'),
            ('change', 'aspects of life that become different over a period of time'),
            ('dynasty', 'a series of rulers from the same family'),
            ('legitimacy', 'the accepted right of a ruler to rule, often based on religion, heredity or law'),
            ('gunpowder empires', 'the Ottoman, Safavid and Mughal empires, which used firearms and artillery to build and hold large states'),
            ('tribute', 'payment made by a weaker state or people to a stronger ruler as a sign of submission')],
     facts=[('In 1453 Constantinople fell to which ruler?', 'Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II', ['Genghis Khan', 'Charlemagne', 'Akbar'], 'The city became the Ottoman capital, Istanbul.'),
            ('Which ruler of the Mali Empire made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, spending so much gold in Cairo that prices fell?', 'Mansa Musa', ['Sundiata Keita', 'Askia Muhammad', 'Shaka Zulu'], 'His wealth made Mali known across the Mediterranean.'),
            ('In which year did Temüjin take the title Genghis Khan?', '1206', ['1066', '1453', '1526'], 'He united the Mongol tribes.'),
            ('Which empire did Charlemagne rule, being crowned emperor by the Pope in 800 CE?', 'The Carolingian (Frankish) empire', ['The Ottoman Empire', 'The Mughal Empire', 'The Mali Empire'], 'The coronation linked his rule to the Church.')],
     why=[('Explain why religion was important for the legitimacy of medieval rulers.', 'Rulers claimed to rule by the will of God or as protectors of the faith – crowned by the Pope, as caliph or sultan, or with the support of religious scholars – so obeying them became a religious duty.'),
          ('Explain why gunpowder weapons helped the Ottoman and Mughal empires grow.', 'Expensive cannons and trained musketeers gave rulers who could pay for them a decisive edge over rivals, and artillery broke walls, as at Constantinople in 1453 and at Panipat in 1526.'),
          ('Explain why controlling trade routes increased the power of empires such as Mali.', 'Taxing trade in gold and salt across the Sahara gave rulers wealth to pay armies and officials, and trade cities such as Timbuktu became centres of learning.'),
          ('Explain why large medieval empires often broke apart after the death of a strong ruler.', 'Power depended on personal loyalty to the ruler; disputed successions, powerful regional governors and long distances made it hard for weaker heirs to keep control.')],
     contrast=[('continuity', 'change', 'Continuity is what stays the same over time (e.g. hereditary monarchy); change is what becomes different (e.g. the use of gunpowder armies).'),
               ('the Mongol Empire', 'the Mughal Empire', 'The Mongol Empire was a vast 13th-century conquest state ruled by nomadic khans that soon split into khanates; the Mughal Empire was a settled, agrarian empire in South Asia with a salaried bureaucracy and a Persianate court that lasted about two centuries.')],
     misc=[('Medieval empires had nothing in common.', 'Most shared features: hereditary monarchy, religious legitimacy, land-based revenue and dependence on armies and loyal elites.'),
           ('Change in history always means progress.', 'Change can bring decline as well as improvement – e.g. the collapse of the Mongol khanates or famine after wars.'),
           ('The Mali Empire was isolated from the rest of the world.', 'It traded gold and salt across the Sahara and was linked to North Africa and the Islamic world; Mansa Musa\'s pilgrimage made it famous in Europe.')],
     cases=[('the fall of Constantinople in 1453', 'Mehmed II used giant cannon to breach the city\'s walls, ending the Byzantine Empire after more than 1 000 years.', 'change in power through new military technology', 'gunpowder artillery shifted power to states that could afford it.'),
            ('Mansa Musa\'s pilgrimage of 1324', 'He travelled to Mecca with a vast caravan and gave away so much gold in Cairo that its value fell for years.', 'the sources of an empire\'s power', 'control of gold and trade routes made Mali wealthy and showed its ruler\'s piety and status.')],
     evaluate=[('Between 1200 and 1600 there was more continuity than change in how empires were ruled.', ['hereditary monarchy and religious legitimacy continued in almost all empires', 'land revenue remained the main source of state income'], ['gunpowder transformed warfare and the size of states', 'bureaucracies such as the mansabdari and Ottoman systems became more organised'], 'Mostly continuity in the foundations of power, but important change in the tools of power – armies, bureaucracy and the scale of states.')],
     src=[('Traveller\'s account (paraphrased)', 'The traveller wrote that the people of Mali had a strong sense of justice and that their sultan did not forgive anyone guilty of injustice. Travellers and residents could move around the country safely without fear of robbers.', 'Paraphrase of Ibn Battuta, "Rihla" (Travels), describing his visit to Mali in 1352–1353', 'to record his travels and describe distant lands for readers in North Africa', 'An eyewitness account by an experienced traveller who visited the court, giving an outsider\'s view of order in Mali.', 'He spent only a few months there, and elsewhere in the account he criticised local customs, so he judged by his own cultural standards.', 'Mali was a well-governed and safe empire with a just ruler.')],
     rq=[('How did the methods used by Ottoman sultans to control their empire change between 1453 and 1566?', 'Which empire was the best?', 'it is a vague value judgement with no criteria', 'It investigates continuity and change in power, with clear dates (Mehmed II to Suleiman I) and plenty of sources.', 'Read two secondary sources; compile a timeline of reforms (devshirme, janissaries, law codes of Suleiman); compare with earlier methods; record evidence in a table; cite in MLA; evaluate the limits of English-language sources.')])
C.chronology(K, 'ch05-st4', c='C3', d=(1, 3), events=[('Charlemagne is crowned emperor', 800), ('Temüjin becomes Genghis Khan', 1206), ('Mansa Musa\'s pilgrimage to Mecca', 1324), ('Ibn Battuta visits Mali (approx.)', 1352), ('the fall of Constantinople', 1453), ('the First Battle of Panipat', 1526), ('Suleiman the Magnificent becomes sultan', 1520), ('the Battle of Chaldiran between Ottomans and Safavids', 1514)])

# =============================================================================================
# ch06 Exploration and Colonialism
# =============================================================================================
K.st('ch06-st1', 'the motives and voyages of European exploration',
     terms=[('Age of Exploration', 'the period from the 15th to the 17th century when European states sent expeditions across the oceans'),
            ('caravel', 'a small, fast Portuguese sailing ship with lateen sails that could sail closer to the wind'),
            ('astrolabe', 'an instrument used to work out latitude by measuring the height of the Sun or a star above the horizon'),
            ('circumnavigation', 'sailing all the way around the world'),
            ('Treaty of Tordesillas', 'the 1494 agreement that divided newly found lands outside Europe between Spain and Portugal along a line west of the Cape Verde Islands'),
            ('"God, gold and glory"', 'a summary of the religious, economic and personal or national motives of European explorers')],
     facts=[('In which year did Christopher Columbus first reach the Caribbean?', '1492', ['1453', '1498', '1519'], 'He landed in the Bahamas in October 1492.'),
            ('Which explorer reached Calicut in India by sea in 1498?', 'Vasco da Gama', ['Ferdinand Magellan', 'Bartolomeu Dias', 'Christopher Columbus'], 'He sailed round the Cape of Good Hope.'),
            ('Who completed the first circumnavigation in 1522 after Magellan was killed in the Philippines?', 'Juan Sebastián Elcano', ['Francis Drake', 'Amerigo Vespucci', 'Henry the Navigator'], 'Only one ship, the Victoria, returned.'),
            ('Which country sponsored Columbus\'s voyages?', 'Spain (Castile)', ['Portugal', 'England', 'Venice'], 'Ferdinand and Isabella funded him.'),
            ('Which Chinese admiral led seven great voyages to the Indian Ocean between 1405 and 1433?', 'Zheng He', ['Zhang Qian', 'Kublai Khan', 'Cai Lun'], 'His fleets reached East Africa.')],
     why=[('Explain why Portugal and Spain wanted to find a sea route to Asia in the 15th century.', 'Spices, silk and other Asian goods reached Europe overland through Ottoman and Venetian middlemen who added high prices; a direct sea route would bring huge profits and bypass rivals.'),
          ('Explain why new technology made long ocean voyages possible.', 'Caravels with lateen sails could sail against the wind, the magnetic compass and astrolabe helped navigation, and better maps and knowledge of winds let sailors cross open ocean.'),
          ('Explain why religion was a motive for European exploration.', 'Rulers and the Church wanted to spread Christianity and saw expeditions as a continuation of wars against Muslim states; papal bulls approved conquests in return for conversion.'),
          ('Explain why the Treaty of Tordesillas mattered for later history.', 'It gave Portugal lands east of the line, including Brazil and the route to Asia, and Spain most of the Americas, which is why Brazil speaks Portuguese and most of Latin America Spanish; other powers ignored it.')],
     contrast=[('the voyages of Zheng He', 'the voyages of Vasco da Gama', 'Zheng He\'s huge Ming fleets (1405–1433) displayed Chinese power and collected tribute but were then stopped; da Gama\'s small fleet (1497–1499) sought trade and led to Portuguese forts and a long-lasting commercial empire in the Indian Ocean.'),
               ('an economic motive', 'a political motive', 'An economic motive is about wealth and trade (spices, gold); a political motive is about the power and prestige of a state or ruler relative to its rivals.')],
     misc=[('Columbus proved the Earth was round.', 'Educated Europeans had known the Earth was round since ancient Greek times; Columbus underestimated its size and believed he had reached Asia.'),
           ('Magellan sailed all the way around the world.', 'Magellan was killed at Mactan in the Philippines in 1521; Elcano and 17 survivors completed the voyage in 1522.'),
           ('Europeans discovered empty lands.', 'The Americas, Africa and Asia were home to millions of people with their own states and societies; "discovery" reflects a European perspective.')],
     cases=[('Vasco da Gama\'s voyage to India (1497–1499)', 'He sailed round the Cape of Good Hope, used an Arab or Gujarati pilot to cross the Indian Ocean and reached Calicut, returning with spices.', 'the economic motives of exploration', 'the voyage opened a direct sea route that allowed Portugal to profit from the spice trade.'),
            ('the Treaty of Tordesillas (1494)', 'Spain and Portugal agreed to divide new lands along a line 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands.', 'political rivalry and power', 'Iberian rulers used diplomacy and papal support to claim lands already inhabited by other peoples.')],
     evaluate=[('"Gold" was the most important motive for European exploration.', ['the spice trade and gold promised huge profits', 'merchants and investors funded voyages for profit'], ['spreading Christianity was a sincere motive for rulers like Isabella', 'national rivalry and personal fame (glory) drove kings and captains'], 'Economic gain was the strongest motive overall, but it was intertwined with religious and political aims that rulers used to justify expeditions.')],
     src=[('Ship\'s journal (paraphrased)', 'The admiral wrote that the people he met were gentle and generous and would easily become Christians. He added that with fifty men they could all be kept under control and made to do whatever was wanted, and that he hoped to find where the gold they wore came from.', 'Paraphrase of the journal of Christopher Columbus, October 1492, surviving in a later summary by Bartolomé de las Casas', 'to report to the Spanish monarchs on the voyage and encourage further support', 'It is an early record of first contact and shows Columbus\'s aims – conversion, control and gold.', 'It survives only in a later copy and summary, and Columbus wrote to impress his sponsors; it gives no Taíno perspective.', 'Columbus saw the Taíno as people to convert and control, and was searching for gold.')],
     rq=[('Why did Portugal, rather than Spain, reach India by sea first, between 1415 and 1498?', 'Who was the best explorer?', 'it is an opinion question without criteria and ignores causes', 'It investigates causation (geography, technology, royal sponsorship) over a clear period with available sources.', 'Construct a timeline of Portuguese voyages from Ceuta (1415) to Calicut (1498); read two secondary sources; note evidence under headings (sponsorship, technology, geography); evaluate perspectives; cite sources.')])
C.chronology(K, 'ch06-st1', c='C3', d=(1, 3), events=[('the Portuguese capture of Ceuta', 1415), ('Bartolomeu Dias rounds the Cape of Good Hope', 1488), ('Columbus reaches the Caribbean', 1492), ('the Treaty of Tordesillas', 1494), ('Vasco da Gama reaches Calicut', 1498), ('Cabral reaches Brazil', 1500), ('Magellan\'s fleet leaves Spain', 1519), ('Elcano completes the circumnavigation', 1522), ('Zheng He\'s first voyage', 1405)])
C.longitude_time(K, 'ch06-st1', c='C3', d=(3, 4))

K.st('ch06-st2', 'the Columbian Exchange and its impacts on people and environments',
     terms=[('Columbian Exchange', 'the transfer of plants, animals, people, diseases and ideas between the Americas and Afro-Eurasia after 1492; the term was coined by historian Alfred Crosby in 1972'),
            ('epidemic', 'a sudden, widespread outbreak of an infectious disease'),
            ('immunity', 'the body\'s ability to resist a disease, often gained from previous exposure'),
            ('cash crop', 'a crop grown to sell for profit rather than for the farmer\'s own use'),
            ('plantation', 'a large estate growing one cash crop, such as sugar, tobacco or cotton, using a large labour force'),
            ('demographic collapse', 'a very large and rapid fall in population')],
     facts=[('Which of these crops travelled from the Americas to the rest of the world?', 'Potatoes', ['Wheat', 'Sugar cane', 'Rice'], 'Maize, tomatoes, cacao and chillies also came from the Americas.'),
            ('Which animal, brought by Europeans, transformed the way of life of peoples of the Great Plains?', 'The horse', ['The llama', 'The turkey', 'The bison'], 'Horses had become extinct in the Americas thousands of years earlier.'),
            ('Which disease did most to cause the collapse of Indigenous populations in the Americas?', 'Smallpox', ['Malaria', 'Cholera', 'Influenza in 1918'], 'Indigenous Americans had no prior exposure.'),
            ('In which year did a smallpox epidemic devastate the Aztec capital Tenochtitlan?', '1520', ['1492', '1600', '1453'], 'It weakened the city before its fall in 1521.'),
            ('Chilli peppers, now central to Indian cooking, originally came from…', 'the Americas', ['Persia', 'China', 'East Africa'], 'Portuguese traders brought them to India in the 16th century.')],
     why=[('Explain why European diseases killed so many Indigenous Americans.', 'Indigenous Americans had never been exposed to Old World diseases such as smallpox and measles, so they had no immunity; epidemics spread ahead of colonists, and war, famine and forced labour made people more vulnerable.'),
          ('Explain why American crops led to population growth in parts of Europe, Africa and Asia.', 'Potatoes, maize and sweet potatoes produce many calories per hectare and grow in poor soils, so they increased food supplies – e.g. the potato in Ireland and northern Europe and maize in Africa and China.'),
          ('Explain why the Columbian Exchange is linked to the Atlantic slave trade.', 'Sugar, tobacco and later cotton plantations in the Americas needed huge labour forces; as Indigenous populations collapsed, Europeans forcibly transported enslaved Africans to work them.'),
          ('Explain why the Columbian Exchange changed environments in the Americas.', 'Introduced cattle, pigs, sheep and horses grazed and spread across the land, Old World weeds and crops replaced native vegetation, and plantations cleared forests.')],
     contrast=[('goods that travelled from the Americas', 'goods that travelled to the Americas', 'From the Americas came maize, potatoes, tomatoes, cacao, tobacco and chillies; to the Americas came wheat, sugar cane, coffee, horses, cattle, pigs and also Old World diseases.'),
               ('a positive impact of the Columbian Exchange', 'a negative impact of the Columbian Exchange', 'A positive impact was more varied and plentiful food worldwide; a negative impact was the death of millions of Indigenous Americans from disease and the expansion of slavery.')],
     misc=[('The Columbian Exchange only moved goods from Europe to the Americas.', 'Exchange went in both directions and involved Africa and Asia too – American crops spread across the world.'),
           ('Indigenous Americans died mainly in battles with Europeans.', 'Disease killed far more than warfare, although violence, forced labour and displacement also caused many deaths.'),
           ('Tomatoes have always been part of Italian food.', 'Tomatoes came from the Americas and only became common in Italian cooking from the 17th–18th centuries.')],
     cases=[('the potato in Ireland', 'By the 1840s many poor Irish families depended almost entirely on the potato; when blight struck in 1845–1849, about a million people died and over a million emigrated.', 'the long-term impact of new crops', 'an American crop allowed population growth but also created dangerous dependence on a single food.'),
            ('the fall of Tenochtitlan (1521)', 'A smallpox epidemic in 1520 killed many inhabitants, including the emperor Cuitláhuac, before Cortés and his Indigenous allies captured the city.', 'disease as part of the Columbian Exchange', 'Old World disease weakened powerful American states and helped small European forces conquer them.')],
     evaluate=[('The Columbian Exchange did more harm than good.', ['disease caused the deaths of tens of millions of Indigenous people', 'it was linked to slavery, colonisation and environmental change'], ['new crops improved diets and allowed population growth across the world', 'it created global trade and modern cuisines'], 'For the peoples of the Americas and Africa it was overwhelmingly harmful; its benefits were real but spread unequally and mainly later.')],
     src=[('Historian\'s book (paraphrased)', 'The historian argued that the most important results of Columbus\'s voyages were biological rather than political: the transfer of plants, animals and germs between hemispheres reshaped diets, landscapes and populations across the world more than any treaty or battle.', 'Paraphrase of Alfred W. Crosby, "The Columbian Exchange: Biological and Cultural Consequences of 1492", 1972', 'to put forward a new interpretation of the impact of 1492 for historians and students', 'It is the work that defined the concept, based on wide research across disciplines.', 'As a secondary interpretation it emphasises biology and may underplay human decisions such as conquest and slavery; newer research has revised some population estimates.', 'The biological exchange after 1492 changed the world more than political events did.')],
     rq=[('How did the arrival of maize change diets and farming in West Africa between 1550 and 1750?', 'Was Columbus good or bad?', 'it is a simplistic moral judgement about one person, not a researchable question', 'It examines a specific global impact of the Columbian Exchange in a defined region and period.', 'Find two secondary sources and a database of crop histories; record evidence of maize cultivation and diet; map its spread; compare with other crops (cassava); evaluate the limits of evidence; cite sources.')])
