K.st('ch06-st3', 'the Atlantic slave trade: its causes, experiences and abolition',
     terms=[('Middle Passage', 'the forced voyage of enslaved Africans across the Atlantic to the Americas, the second leg of the triangular trade'),
            ('triangular trade', 'the Atlantic trade pattern: manufactured goods from Europe to West Africa, enslaved people to the Americas, and plantation goods back to Europe'),
            ('chattel slavery', 'a system in which enslaved people were treated as property that could be bought, sold and inherited'),
            ('abolition', 'the ending of the slave trade or of slavery itself by law'),
            ('resistance', 'actions taken by enslaved people to oppose slavery, from running away and sabotage to revolts'),
            ('abolitionist', 'a person who campaigned to end the slave trade and slavery')],
     facts=[('According to the SlaveVoyages database, about how many Africans were forced onto ships in the transatlantic slave trade between 1501 and 1866?', 'About 12.5 million', ['About 1 million', 'About 50 million', 'About 125 000'], 'About 10.7 million survived the voyage.'),
            ('In which year did the British Parliament abolish the slave trade?', '1807', ['1776', '1833', '1865'], 'Slavery itself was abolished in most British colonies by an act of 1833.'),
            ('Which revolution, beginning in 1791, created the first independent state founded by formerly enslaved people?', 'The Haitian Revolution', ['The American Revolution', 'The French Revolution', 'The Mexican Revolution'], 'Haiti became independent in 1804.'),
            ('Which country was the last in the Americas to abolish slavery, in 1888?', 'Brazil', ['The United States', 'Cuba', 'Jamaica'], 'The Lei Áurea (Golden Law) ended slavery.'),
            ('Which formerly enslaved man published his autobiography in London in 1789?', 'Olaudah Equiano', ['Frederick Douglass', 'Toussaint Louverture', 'Nelson Mandela'], 'It was widely read by abolitionists.')],
     why=[('Explain why European traders turned to enslaved African labour in the Americas.', 'Plantations of sugar, tobacco and later cotton needed huge, cheap labour forces; Indigenous populations had collapsed from disease, and European traders could buy captives from some African states in exchange for goods.'),
          ('Explain why the Middle Passage had such a high death rate.', 'Captives were chained in crowded, airless holds for weeks with little food or water, so diseases such as dysentery spread, and violence and despair also caused deaths – around one in seven died on average.'),
          ('Explain why the British slave trade was abolished in 1807.', 'Campaigns by abolitionists (including formerly enslaved people such as Equiano), petitions, boycotts of slave-grown sugar, fear of revolts after Haiti and changing economic interests combined to persuade Parliament.'),
          ('Explain why the Haitian Revolution alarmed slave-holding powers.', 'Enslaved people defeated French, Spanish and British forces and founded an independent state, showing that slavery could be overthrown from within.')],
     contrast=[('abolition of the slave trade', 'abolition of slavery', 'Abolishing the trade (Britain 1807, USA 1808) stopped the buying and shipping of new captives; abolishing slavery (British colonies from 1834, USA 1865, Brazil 1888) freed people who were already enslaved.'),
               ('active resistance', 'passive resistance', 'Active resistance includes revolts and escape (e.g. the Haitian Revolution, maroon communities); passive resistance includes working slowly, damaging tools and keeping culture, language and religion alive.')],
     misc=[('Enslaved Africans accepted their situation without resistance.', 'Resistance was constant: shipboard revolts, escape to maroon communities, sabotage, preserving culture and large uprisings such as Haiti (1791) and the Baptist War in Jamaica (1831–32).'),
           ('Slavery ended everywhere in 1807.', '1807 ended the British slave trade only; slavery continued in British colonies until 1834–38, in the USA until 1865 and in Brazil until 1888.'),
           ('Abolition happened only because white politicians changed their minds.', 'Formerly enslaved writers and campaigners, revolts by enslaved people and economic changes were all crucial.')],
     cases=[('Olaudah Equiano\'s autobiography (1789)', 'Equiano described his capture as a child, the Middle Passage and life under slavery, and his book went through many editions.', 'experiences of the slave trade and the abolition campaign', 'a first-hand account by a formerly enslaved person gave the campaign powerful evidence and a human voice.'),
            ('the Haitian Revolution (1791–1804)', 'Enslaved people in Saint-Domingue rose up; under leaders such as Toussaint Louverture they defeated colonial armies and declared independence in 1804.', 'resistance and abolition', 'it showed enslaved people as agents of their own freedom and influenced debates on abolition elsewhere.')],
     evaluate=[('The actions of enslaved people were the most important reason for the abolition of slavery.', ['revolts such as Haiti and Jamaica made slavery costly and dangerous', 'testimony by formerly enslaved people shaped public opinion'], ['abolitionist campaigns in Britain and the USA built political pressure', 'economic changes reduced the importance of slave-grown sugar to Britain'], 'A strong argument: resistance was central, but abolition came from the interaction of resistance, campaigns and economic and political change.')],
     src=[('Autobiography (paraphrased)', 'The author recalled being taken on board the ship as a child and placed below deck, where the heat, the crowding and the smell were so terrible that many fell sick and died. He wrote that he wished for death to end his misery.', 'Paraphrase of Olaudah Equiano, "The Interesting Narrative of the Life of Olaudah Equiano", London, 1789', 'to describe his life and to persuade British readers to support the abolition of the slave trade', 'It is a rare first-hand account by someone who experienced enslavement, and it shows how abolitionists used personal testimony.', 'It was written decades after the events for a campaign; some historians have debated where Equiano was born, although the conditions he describes are confirmed by many other sources.', 'The Middle Passage was horrific, with overcrowding, disease and death.')],
     rq=[('How did the testimony of formerly enslaved people contribute to the British campaign to abolish the slave trade between 1787 and 1807?', 'Why was slavery bad?', 'it is a closed moral question whose answer is obvious; it does not investigate evidence', 'It examines the role of different groups in historical change, with primary sources available online (e.g. the National Archives).', 'Read Equiano\'s and Cugoano\'s narratives (extracts); study petitions and parliamentary records; read two secondary sources; record evidence by group; evaluate the purposes of each source; cite in MLA.')])
